.TH IO_ADDBUF_INTERNAL 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
iob_addbuf_internal \- Internal function to add data to an I/O batch buffer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/iob_internal.h>

.IB int
.IB iob_addbuf_internal(
.IB io_batch* b,
.IB const void* buf,
.IB uint64 n,
.IB void (*cleanup)(struct iob_entry* x)
.IB );

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBiob_addbuf_internal\fR function is an internal function used by \fBiob_addbuf\fR and \fBiob_addbuf_free\fR to add data from a buffer to an I/O batch buffer. It takes additional parameters for cleanup functions and is responsible for the actual allocation and initialization of the batch entry.

The function takes the following parameters:

.IB b
Pointer to the I/O batch buffer.

.IB buf
Pointer to the buffer containing the data to be added.

.IB n
Number of bytes to add from the buffer.

.IB cleanup
Pointer to a cleanup function that will be called when the batch entry is no longer needed.

The function returns 0 on success, or a negative value indicating an error.

The function is part of the \fBiob\fR library, which provides a framework for managing I/O operations in batch mode.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBiob_addbuf_internal\fR function directly, although it is typically called indirectly through \fBiob_addbuf\fR or \fBiob_addbuf_free\fR:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/iob_internal.h>

void cleanup_example(struct iob_entry* x) {
  free((char*)x->buf);
}

int main() {
  io_batch batch;
  char* data = malloc(14);
  if (data == NULL) {
    perror("malloc");
    return 1;
  }

  strcpy(data, "Hello, World!");

  io_batch_init(&batch);

  iob_entry* entry = array_allocate(&batch.b, sizeof(iob_entry), array_length(&batch.b, sizeof(iob_entry)));
  if(!entry) {
    free(data);
    return 1;
  }

  entry->type = FROMBUF;
  entry->fd = -1;
  entry->buf = data;
  entry->n = 14;
  entry->offset = 0;
  entry->cleanup = cleanup_example;

  batch.bytesleft += 14;
  batch.bufs++;

  if (iob_execute(&batch) != 0) {
    perror("iob_execute");
    iob_free(&batch);
    return 1;
  }

  printf("Data added to batch buffer\n");

  iob_free(&batch);
  return 0;
}
.nef